Fillets: two stripes joined across a corner of the solid (0772) make one
face each over the corner, as the stored designs' roundings do, instead
of three (mitcad#343).

0772 walked the joined blend from the middle of the first stripe's last
piece to a section inside the second's: the first stripe kept its last
piece's face up to the walk's start, the walk made a second face and the
second stripe's last piece a third; the walked face was generated by no
edge (the stripes' faces were named after their pieces' own surfaces). The
stored bodies have two faces per corner, one of each edge, the first's
holding the cut by the first side, the second's the cuts by the corner's
face and by the second side; a later rounding of the sides' edges names
them by those faces.

- ChFi3d_Builder::JoinAcrossCorner walks the blend from the first
  stripe's last piece's far end (where the walk starts on the faces'
  surfaces: the plane unbounded, the other face's surface within its own
  period about the face, else widened by its size and ten radii: the
  contacts at the pieces' far ends lie past their faces) to the second's
  far end (its guide through both last pieces' ball centres, the second
  piece's taken up to its spine end). The two last pieces are replaced:
  the first stripe takes the walk's pieces up to the section through the
  junction on the first side, the second the rest, on a copy of the walked
  surface (one face each), its pieces' lines and p-curves taken in the
  second stripe's spine parameter (reversed where it runs the other way)
  and its sides swapped where its faces are in the other order. The two
  stripes end on that section, their shared edge. The far ends keep the
  last pieces' points, at their parameters on the walked lines, for the
  corners there; the faces generated by the stripes' edges are the walked
  surface and its copy.
- JoinAcrossCorners starts the walk on the stripe whose edge runs out of
  the corner where the other's runs into it, whichever order the edges
  were given in (the stored bodies' faces meet at that stripe's junction).
- PerformIntersectionAtEnd: where a corner extends the last piece's
  surface to meet the face at the end, the stripe's other pieces on the
  same surface take the extended surface too (one face with it; the
  surface is the same within its old bounds).

Upstream: new.
